{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Announcing Amazon EKS Capabilities - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has announced the general availability of EKS Capabilities, a set of fully-managed, Kubernetes-native platform features designed for workload deployment, AWS cloud resource management, and Kubernetes resource composition and orchestration. EKS Capabilities improve platform performance and security by offloading operations to AWS, enabling faster application deployment and scaling. The capabilities run on AWS-owned infrastructure, with AWS handling auto scaling, patching, and upgrading. Application developers can utilize ready-to-use platform capabilities, while platform teams can offload operational tasks to AWS. Three capabilities are available at launch: continuous deployment with Argo CD, AWS resource management through AWS Controllers for Kubernetes (ACK), and dynamic resource orchestration using Kube Resource Orchestrator (KRO). EKS Capabilities is available in all AWS Regions except AWS GovCloud (US) and China Regions. Users can enable EKS Capabilities in a new or existing EKS cluster using the EKS API, CLI, eksctl, AWS Console, or infrastructure as code tooling. For more information, visit the EKS Capabilities feature webpage, user guide, and pricing webpage","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-eks-capabilities/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"amazon-keyspaces","skill_description":"Provides authoritative compatibility checks, pricing estimates, connection troubleshooting, pre-warming guidance, and infrastructure mutations for Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra). Covers LWT/batch operations, secondary indexes, materialized views, capacity modes, TTL, PITR, CDC, auto-scaling, multi-region keyspaces, UDTs, nodetool diagnostics parsing, SQL-to-Cassandra migration, and Cassandra-to-Keyspaces migration scenarios. Agents frequently produce incomplete or incorrect answers about Keyspaces feature support without this skill loaded.\n\nServices: keyspaces, cassandra\nTasks: cost-estimation, migration-assessment, compatibility-check, data-modeling, create, modify\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, dba\nWorkload: nosql, wide-column-store, cassandra-migration\n*","skill_name":"amazon-keyspaces"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"managing-amazon-msk","skill_description":"Operates Amazon MSK Provisioned clusters (Standard and Express brokers). Required for ANY MSK Provisioned task — training data conflates Standard and Express. Covers performance, consumer lag, storage, traffic shaping; sizing Standard vs Express; Kafka client tuning; CloudWatch alarms; cluster configs; maintenance, patching, rolling restarts; topic management; MSK Replicator setup for DR and active-active/active-passive replication (same-/cross-account), including throughput and partition sizing; Streaming Tables for S3 Tables and Data Delivery for General Purpose S3 — setup, IAM, monitoring. Prefer Flink for Kafka Iceberg sink questions. Migrations: use migrate-to-msk. Triggers: MSK Provisioned, Kafka, `kafka.*`/`express.*` types, AWS/Kafka namespace, cross-cluster replication, Kafka to Iceberg on S3 Tables, Kafka to S3, lakehouse, data lake from Kafka, Kafka Connect S3 Sink or Firehose alternative. DO NOT USE for MSK Connect — search docs instead. Serverless: only S3 Tables/data delivery eligibility.\n\n\nServices: msk, kafka, cloudwatch\nTasks: troubleshoot, debug, optimize, deploy, monitor, replicate, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: streaming, data-analytics","skill_name":"managing-amazon-msk"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CIS EKS Benchmark control titles", "parent_id": "3423aa95-dae9-4ee2-b9e4-86fe8827fa8b"}

